{"requirements":[{"id":"https-8443","requirement":"node1 serves HTTPS on TCP port 8443.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service was active and enabled, listened on 0.0.0.0:8443, returned a successful CA-validated HTTPS response, and served the same certificate serial stored on disk. These results persisted after reboot, with runtime and permanent firewall access enabled.","evidence_ids":["cmd-842d61534bff4ca9","cmd-b55daed42624443a","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"automatic-renewal","requirement":"The certificate is automatically renewed before expiration.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The renewal implementation uses a seven-day pre-expiry threshold. A one-day certificate was replaced with a new 30-day certificate, and the persistent, enabled twice-daily timer had a scheduled next activation after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-162002310977407d","cmd-25f827b31282454a","cmd-b55daed42624443a"]},{"id":"continuous-certificate-adoption","requirement":"The HTTPS service picks up each renewed certificate while continuing to answer requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"During a forced renewal, the certificate serial changed and the expiration extended while the service PID remained 1739. All 80 concurrent HTTPS requests succeeded with zero failures, and a final validated response reported the new serial.","evidence_ids":["cmd-25f827b31282454a"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.99,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Persistent scripts, account, units, certificate storage, and firewall changes directly supported the required service. The initial certificate-directory permission prevented TLS operation, but it was corrected and the service retested successfully; this was a necessary corrective action rather than an unrelated experiment. The forced short-lived certificate was bounded validation state and was immediately replaced by the tested renewal path. Temporary test files were trap-cleaned, no failed services remained, and package health stayed clean. The required restart used transient systemd units and left no observed unwanted residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-162002310977407d","cmd-f9b4496e4ba24509","cmd-13bba33232ac4257","cmd-5e664e353eba4d6c","cmd-f9dcb6fbc5ac468f","cmd-25e46b9b9ce44003","cmd-4138ff213a3a495b","cmd-25f827b31282454a","cmd-6f72babd350542dd","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. HTTPS operated on port 8443, the renewal mechanism replaced a near-expiry certificate, and the running service adopted it without a failed request or PID change. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and the service, certificate state, timer, listener, and firewall recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the reboot.","limitations":["The near-expiry renewal service was deliberately invoked with systemctl rather than waiting for the timer's next natural activation; automatic operation is supported by the enabled persistent timer and observed schedule, but no timer-triggered near-expiry rotation was captured.","HTTPS requests were tested from node1 against its managed IP; no independent external-client probe was captured.","There is no cluster state to validate because the managed topology contains only node1."]}
